<apex:page showHeader="false" sidebar="false" standardStylesheets="false"
           applyHtmlTag="false" applyBodyTag="false" docType="html-5.0"
           title="Privacy | Curb Cut" cache="true" expires="300">
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8"/>
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1"/>
  <meta name="description" content="What Curb Cut collects, what deliberately does not exist in the system, and who processes it."/>
  <title>Privacy | Curb Cut</title>
</head>
<body>
<c:CurbCutShell pageTitle="Privacy" current="privacy"
  eyebrow="Privacy Policy, updated 30 August 2026"
  heading="What we hold, and what does not exist."
  lede="The safest way to protect something is not to collect it. Most of this is about what there is nowhere to put.">

  <section aria-labelledby="sec-what-does-not-exist">
    <div class="plain">
      <span class="tag">The short version</span>
      <p>
        We keep a scrambled version of your phone number, what you tell us is
        hard at work, and anything you ask us to remember. There is no field for
        a diagnosis, so we could not store one if we tried.
      </p>
      <p>
        We never sell it, never share it for anyone else's purposes, and never
        use it to advertise to you. You can see what is held, turn it off, or
        have it deleted, at any time, without giving a reason.
      </p>
      <p>The rest of this page says the same thing more carefully.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="sec-what-does-not-exist-2">
    <h2 id="sec-what-does-not-exist-2">id="sec-what-does-not-exist">What does not exist here</h2>
    <div class="note absent">
      <p>
        There is no field anywhere in this system for a <strong>diagnosis</strong>,
        <strong>condition</strong>, <strong>disability type</strong>,
        <strong>medical note</strong>, <strong>severity</strong> or
        <strong>prognosis</strong>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Not hidden, not locked, not encrypted &mdash; absent. An automated check
        refuses to let the software be built if anyone ever adds such a field.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you mention a condition anyway, it is taken out of the text before
        anything is saved, and you are told so in the same reply. What is left is
        the part about what is hard, which is the part we work from.
      </p>
      <p>
        We will not overstate this. That removal runs against a list of
        conditions we recognise, and no list can cover every word in every
        language. It is a real safeguard, not a guarantee, and you deserve to
        know which. What <em>is</em> guaranteed is that there is nowhere in this
        system built to hold a condition, so nothing is ever asked for, indexed,
        reported on, or shown to your employer.
      </p>
      <p>
        Words like <strong>Deaf</strong>, <strong>blind</strong> and
        <strong>hard of hearing</strong> are deliberately left alone. They are
        how people describe themselves and what they need, and the interpreter
        routing depends on reading them.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="sec-what-we-do-keep">
    <h2 id="sec-what-we-do-keep">What we do keep</h2>
    <table class="facts">
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">A scrambled phone number</th>
          <td>So your conversation carries on across messages. Your number is
              turned into an irreversible code the moment it arrives. The number
              itself is never saved and never written to our ledger.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">What you say is hard</th>
          <td>In your own words, so we can look up what might help. What is
              difficult, never why it is difficult.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Anything you ask us to remember</th>
          <td>A standing preference, in your exact words, and who you chose to
              let see it.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Requests you approved</th>
          <td>So the request you agreed to can be sent, and so you can see what
              came back.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Every time a preference was shown</th>
          <td>So you can see it. Something that travels ahead of you into a new
              team is a disclosure made while you were not in the room. You are
              entitled to know it happened.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>Conversations are anonymous unless you choose otherwise.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="sec-what-we-never-do">
    <h2 id="sec-what-we-never-do">What we never do</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>We never sell your information.</strong> Not to anyone, at any
          price.</li>
      <li><strong>Your mobile information stays here.</strong> No mobile
          information, phone number, or text-message opt-in consent is shared
          with, sold to, or rented to any third party or affiliate for their
          marketing or promotional purposes &mdash; not ever, and not in any
          form. The only parties that touch it are the carrier that delivers the
          message and the platform that runs the assistant, both named below,
          and both only in order to deliver the reply you asked for.</li>
      <li><strong>We never hand it to anyone for their own purposes.</strong>
          No data brokers, no advertisers, no analytics companies.</li>
      <li><strong>We never advertise to you.</strong> No promotional messages are
          sent at all. Every message you get is a reply to one you sent.</li>
      <li><strong>We never guess things about you.</strong> Nothing is profiled
          or predicted from your behaviour. Every signal in the system is one you
          set yourself, in your own words.</li>
      <li><strong>We never tell your employer anything you did not approve</strong>,
          in the conversation, at the time.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="sec-who-else-touches-it">
    <h2 id="sec-who-else-touches-it">Who else touches it</h2>
    <p>
      Two companies handle data so the service can work at all. Neither may use
      it for their own ends.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Twilio</strong> carries the messages and calls, and necessarily
          handles your phone number to deliver them, as any phone company must.</li>
      <li><strong>Salesforce</strong> hosts the database and the assistant.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Three places hold what you typed for a while, and we cannot make them not:
      Salesforce keeps the agent conversation as session data under its own
      retention, Twilio keeps text and voice logs on its side for texts and
      calls, and our relay holds a conversation in memory for thirty minutes.
      What Curb Cut itself saves never includes a condition or a phone number.
    </p>
    <p>
      Information is stored on Salesforce infrastructure in the United States. We
      give it to no one else, except where the law compels it &mdash; and there is
      no medical information in the system to give.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="sec-what-you-can-do">
    <h2 id="sec-what-you-can-do">What you can do, any time</h2>
    <p>
      These work on the web page, by text, and by email. They mean the same thing
      on all three.
    </p>
    <ul class="kwlist">
      <li><span class="kw">HUMAN</span><span>Hand this to a real person, on whichever channel you are already using. You will not be asked to explain it again, and nobody will ring you.</span></li>
      <li><span class="kw">WHO</span><span>See everyone who has been shown a preference of yours. Send it with your code, like <span class="kw">WHO 4KQ7MT</span>.</span></li>
      <li><span class="kw">OFF</span><span>Turn a saved preference off. Immediate, and sent with your code the same way. You will not be asked why &mdash; there is nowhere to record a reason, so nobody can ask you for one later.</span></li>
      <li><span class="kw">DELETE</span><span>Throw away a drafted request before it is sent.</span></li>
      <li><span class="kw">STOP</span><span>Stop receiving messages from the number entirely.</span></li>
    </ul>

    <div class="note">
      <p>
        <strong>Why there is a code.</strong> When you save a preference we give
        you six characters and show them once. We keep only a scrambled version
        of them, which cannot be turned back into the code.
      </p>
      <p>
        That is the price of not holding a file on you. There is no account here,
        no password, and no phone number on record, so there is nothing we could
        look you up by &mdash; and the code is what lets you switch a disclosure
        off from a text message without us ever knowing who you are. If you lose
        it, send <span class="kw">HUMAN</span> and a person will sort it out with
        you.
      </p>
    </div>

    <p>
      Or email <a href="mailto:parth.sevak2@gmail.com">parth.sevak2@gmail.com</a>
      to ask what is held about you, correct it, or have it deleted.
    </p>
    <p>
      Records are kept only while the request or preference they belong to is
      live. An anonymous conversation that leads nowhere leaves nothing behind
      that could identify you.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="sec-if-this-changes">
    <h2 id="sec-if-this-changes">If this changes</h2>
    <p>
      A changed policy appears here with a new date at the top. Carrying on using
      the service after that means the newer version applies.
    </p>
  </section>

</c:CurbCutShell>
</body>
</html>
</apex:page>
